              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 5.24: Demonstration of Tyndall effect in a sports stadium"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.364,
            0.088,
            0.888,
            0.14
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "the dust and smoke particles in the air. You might have also observed this phenomenon in the floodlights in a sports stadium (Fig. 5.24). Think of some more examples!"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.364,
            0.142,
            0.888,
            0.194
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Scattering of light occurs when it passes through a colloid or a suspension but not when it passes through a transparent solution."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.363,
            0.196,
            0.889,
            0.283
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The components of a colloid are called the dispersed phase and the dispersion medium. The solute-like component or the dispersed particles, in a colloid form the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "dispersed phase",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and the component in which the dispersed phase is suspended is known as the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "dispersion medium",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.073,
            0.282,
            0.138,
            0.317
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 5,
              "bbox": [
                0.073,
                0.282,
                0.138,
                0.317
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_16_image_5.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.29,
            0.364,
            0.308
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Threads of Curiosity"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.079,
            0.323,
            0.864,
            0.433
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Some common colloids we encounter in our daily lives have both the dispersed phase and the dispersion medium as liquids. These are called emulsions. Emulsions containing oil and water are further classified as oil-in-water or water-in-oil, depending on the nature of the dispersed phase and the dispersion medium. Milk and vanishing creams are common examples of oil-in-water emulsions, whereas butter, body lotions, cold cream, etc., are examples of water-in-oil emulsions. Some medicines are prepared as emulsions to disperse them in water and make them easily palatable. This also reduces the greasy feeling of the liquid medicine."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.081,
            0.433,
            0.862,
            0.466
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The presence of emulsifying agents stabilises emulsions. For example, proteins in milk and butter act as emulsifying agents."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.081,
            0.468,
            0.862,
            0.501
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "You can make an emulsion by thoroughly mixing and shaking a few drops of cooking oil with water containing a few drops of soap solution."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.082,
            0.522,
            0.142,
            0.555
          ],
          "content": [
            {
